---
/* Ported from docs/kitchen-sink/pain-points-item.html (CCWEB2-317 phase 3).
 *
 * Shell extraction. Mounting pattern and why it is not an island: button.astro.

 * The page that would fail loudest if the script were processed. Story 3 feeds
 * the component hostile markdown to show SanitizerPort actually sanitising, and
 * that string contains a `<\/script>` — an escape that only means anything to a
 * JavaScript parser reading raw text. Verified after the port: the page's
 * window.__ppXss is still [] once it settles, so nothing in the payload ran. */
import DocPage from '../../layouts/DocPage.astro';
---
<DocPage
  title="PainPointsItem — CODECAVE storybook"
  nav="kitchen-sink"
  stylesheets={['../storybook/tw-bridge.css','storybook.css']}
  importmap
>
<div class="sb">
  <a class="sb-back" href="index.html">&larr; All components</a>

  <header class="sb-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Content · 13 of 13</p>
    <h1>PainPointsItem</h1>
    <p>A fixed-width card whose body is CMS markdown rendered to HTML at
       component scope, with a custom <code>marked</code> renderer supplying the
       Tailwind classes.</p>
    <div class="sb-tags">
      <span class="sb-tag is-vue">Vue island</span>
      <span class="sb-tag">src/components/project/pain-points-item.vue</span>
      <span class="sb-tag">rendered live from compiled/pain-points-item.js</span>
      <span class="sb-tag">v-html</span>
      <span class="sb-tag">sanitized · dompurify 3.4.14</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="sb-section">
    <h2>Props</h2>
    <table class="sb-props">
      <thead><tr><th>Prop</th><th>Type</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>item <span class="sb-req">*</span></td><td><code>Feature</code></td><td>Strapi entity. Reads <code>image.url</code>, <code>image.name</code>, <code>content</code> (markdown).</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">The renderer overrides two node types only:
       <code>paragraph</code> → <code>&lt;p class="text-lg"&gt;</code> and
       <code>list</code> → <code>&lt;ul|ol class="pt-2 pl-5 list-disc"&gt;</code>.
       Everything else falls through to <code>marked</code>'s defaults, unstyled.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="sb-section">
    <h2>Stories</h2>
    <div class="sb-stories">

      <div class="sb-story">
        <div class="sb-canvas is-start is-tall">
          <div id="story-pp1"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="sb-story-body">
          <span class="sb-story-name">Paragraph only</span>
          <span class="sb-story-args">content = one paragraph</span>
          <span class="sb-story-note">18px body on a 24px-radius #0F0F15 card, fixed 14rem wide.
            The markdown really runs through the component's <code>marked</code> instance.</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="sb-story">
        <div class="sb-canvas is-start is-tall">
          <div id="story-pp2"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="sb-story-body">
          <span class="sb-story-name">With a list</span>
          <span class="sb-story-args">content = paragraph + bullets</span>
          <span class="sb-story-note">The custom renderer is why the bullets have discs at all — <code>list-disc</code> is not a browser default under Tailwind's reset.</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="sb-story">
        <div class="sb-canvas is-start is-tall">
          <div id="story-pp3"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="sb-story-body">
          <span class="sb-story-name">Hostile CMS content</span>
          <span class="sb-story-args">content = markdown containing raw HTML</span>
          <span class="sb-story-note">Markdown permits raw HTML and <code>marked</code>
            passes it through, so whatever a CMS editor types reaches
            <code>v-html</code>. This story feeds the component an
            <code>onload</code> handler, a <code>javascript:</code> link and a
            <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag, each of which sets a flag if it ever
            executes. The readout below is generated after mount from the DOM the
            real component produced — nothing here is written by hand. Note the
            icon still appears: sanitising strips the handler, not the image.</span>
          <div class="sb-verdict" id="pp3-verdict"><b>running…</b></div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sb-section">
    <h2>Findings</h2>

    <p class="sb-note"><strong>Fixed upstream — <code>v-html</code> is now sanitized.</strong>
      This card used to parse <code>item.content</code> with <code>marked</code> and
      bind the result straight to <code>v-html</code>. Markdown permits raw HTML and
      <code>marked</code> dropped its own <code>sanitize</code> option in v5, so
      anything an editor could type into the Strapi <code>content</code> field
      executed in every visitor's page — an XSS sink gated only by CMS access
      control. As of the 2026-08-20 resync the component wraps the parse in
      <code>sanitize()</code> from <code>isomorphic-dompurify</code>, which closes it.
      Kept here rather than deleted because the specimen is the record of what the
      component was, and this was the site's only P0.</p>

    <p class="sb-note"><strong>The sanitizer here is real.</strong>
      The storybook resolves <code>isomorphic-dompurify</code> to
      <code>kitchen-sink/ports/sanitizer.adapter.ts</code>, which is plain
      <code>dompurify</code> at the version the isomorphic wrapper itself
      resolves to. The wrapper's only job is pairing DOMPurify with
      <code>jsdom</code> so the call also works during SSR; a docs page is
      always a browser, so the port drops the half that has nothing to do here
      and keeps the engine. Same defaults as the site — no config is passed
      there either. The third story above is the proof rather than this
      paragraph.</p>

    <p class="sb-note is-warn"><strong>Markdown is parsed once, outside any reactive scope.</strong>
      <code>htmlContent</code> is computed at <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> body
      level from <code>props.item.content</code>, not in a <code>computed</code>.
      If the parent swaps <code>item</code> without remounting, the card keeps
      rendering the previous body while the image updates — the two halves of the
      card can disagree.</p>

    <p class="sb-note"><strong>The ordered-list branch still gets <code>list-disc</code>.</strong>
      The renderer picks <code>ol</code> vs <code>ul</code> from
      <code>token.ordered</code> but applies the same class string either way, so a
      numbered list renders with bullets and no numbers.</p>

    <p class="sb-note"><strong>Width is fixed at <code>w-56</code>.</strong>
      14rem regardless of viewport or content, with only the horizontal margin
      responding to breakpoints. Long copy grows the card downward indefinitely,
      which is what the carousel parent expects — but it means the component
      cannot be reused in a wider slot.</p>
  </section>

</div>

<script is:inline type="module">
  import { createApp } from 'vue';
  import PainPointsItem from './compiled/pain-points-item.js';
  import { usePlaceholders } from './placeholders.js';

  createApp(PainPointsItem, {
    item: {
      image: { url: '/uploads/pain-icon.svg', name: 'pain-icon.svg' },
      content: 'Manual exports between Revit and the ERP.',
    },
  }).mount('#story-pp1');

  createApp(PainPointsItem, {
    item: {
      image: { url: '/uploads/pain-icon.svg', name: 'pain-icon.svg' },
      content: 'Three places the data drifts:\n\n- Nightly batch\n- Manual CSV\n- Ad-hoc fixes',
    },
  }).mount('#story-pp2');

  /* ---- story 3: does the sanitizer actually run? --------------------------
   * Anything that executes pushes its name here. If the array is still empty
   * once the page has settled, nothing in the hostile markdown ran. */
  window.__ppXss = [];

  // The <img> points at a real asset and hangs its payload on onload, not
  // onerror: a deliberately broken src would prove the same thing but leave a
  // 404 in the console of a page whose whole job is to be trustworthy on sight.
  const hostile = [
    'Raw HTML pasted into the CMS field:',
    '',
    '<img src="../assets/images/sb-icon.svg" class="w-8 h-8"'
      + ' onload="window.__ppXss.push(\'img onload\')">',
    '<a href="javascript:window.__ppXss.push(\'javascript: href\')">a link</a>',
    '<script>window.__ppXss.push(\'inline script\')<\/script>',
  ].join('\n');

  createApp(PainPointsItem, {
    item: {
      image: { url: '/uploads/pain-icon.svg', name: 'pain-icon.svg' },
      content: hostile,
    },
  }).mount('#story-pp3');

  // Skips the <img> inside story 3's rendered markdown: that one is evidence,
  // and rewriting its src would rewrite the thing under test.
  usePlaceholders('.sb-canvas img', '../assets/images/sb-icon.svg',
    (img) => img.closest('[class*="text-body"]') !== null);

  /* Report on what survived. Read back from the DOM the component rendered
   * rather than re-running the sanitizer, so the readout cannot agree with
   * itself while the component does something else. */
  const report = () => {
    const el = document.querySelector('#story-pp3 [class*="text-body"]');
    const box = document.getElementById('pp3-verdict');
    if (!el || !box) return;

    const html = el.innerHTML.trim();
    const executed = window.__ppXss;
    // Substring checks, not a parse: the question is whether the dangerous text
    // is present at all, and a parser would normalise some of it out of view.
    const survivors = [
      ['onload=', /onload/i],
      ['javascript: href', /javascript:/i],
      ['<script>', /<script/i],
    ].filter(([, re]) => re.test(html)).map(([name]) => name);

    const clean = executed.length === 0 && survivors.length === 0;
    box.className = 'sb-verdict ' + (clean ? 'is-pass' : 'is-fail');
    box.innerHTML = '';

    const head = document.createElement('b');
    head.textContent = clean
      ? 'PASS — nothing executed, and onload / javascript: / <script> are all gone from the DOM.'
      : 'FAIL — ' + [
          executed.length ? 'executed: ' + executed.join(', ') : '',
          survivors.length ? 'still in the DOM: ' + survivors.join(', ') : '',
        ].filter(Boolean).join('; ');
    box.appendChild(head);

    const pre = document.createElement('pre');
    pre.textContent = html;   // textContent, so the readout cannot itself render
    box.appendChild(pre);
  };

  // The image load that would trigger onload is async, so give it the load
  // event plus a margin before declaring nothing happened.
  const schedule = () => setTimeout(report, 400);
  if (document.readyState === 'complete') schedule();
  else window.addEventListener('load', schedule);
</script>
</DocPage>
